\section{Closing the loop with \code{e2tree}}{

\code{e2tree()} takes a dissimilarity \code{D} as an argument, which is the object
\code{\link[=as_dissimilarity]{as_dissimilarity()}} produces, and returns a single tree meant to explain
the ensemble that dissimilarity came from. This method reads that tree back
as a proximity, so the explanation can be compared with what it explains on
the same footing:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{px    <- as_proximity(rf, newdata = data)      # what the forest represents
tree  <- e2tree(y ~ ., data, D = as_dissimilarity(px), ensemble = rf)
mantel_test(px, as_proximity(tree))            # how much of it survives
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

The Mantel correlation between the two is a measure of how much of the
ensemble's geometry the single tree reproduces, which is the question
\code{e2tree} exists to answer and which it cannot ask of itself.
}

\section{Why the name is \code{as_proximity()}}{

\code{e2tree}, which builds an explainable tree on the same similarity structure
and is the package most likely to be attached alongside this one, exports a
\code{proximity()} of its own. Two generics of the same name mask each other in
the order the packages were attached, and the one the user gets is then a
property of their \code{library()} calls rather than of what they asked for. The
\code{as_} prefix also says what the function does: it coerces a fitted ensemble
into an object of class \code{proximity}, the way \code{as.dist()} coerces into a
\code{dist}.
}

